Audio Feature Engineering & Machine Learning PipelineThis repository contains Python scripts and workflows for processing audio data, generating Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) spectrograms, extracting spectral features (such as bandwidth and centroid), and building classification models using librosa and scikit-learn.📋 Overview of Code & Scripts1. Calculating a SpectrogramComputes the short-time Fourier transform from raw audio to evaluate spectral content over time.Pythonfrom librosa.core import stft

# Prepare the STFT
HOP_LENGTH = 2**4
spec = stft(audio, hop_length=HOP_LENGTH, n_fft=2**7)
2. Converting to Decibels and VisualizingTransforms amplitude spectrograms into a decibel scale for optimal visualization and comparison against raw waveforms.Pythonfrom librosa.core import amplitude_to_db
from librosa.display import specshow

# Convert the spectrogram to decibels for visualization
spec_db = amplitude_to_db(spec)

# Plot the spectrogram over time
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 5))
specshow(spec_db, x_axis='time', y_axis='hz', hop_length=HOP_LENGTH, ax=ax)
plt.show()
3. Engineering Spectral Features (Centroid & Bandwidth)Extracts summary spectral features across multiple spectrograms in a loop.Pythonimport librosa as lr
import numpy as np

bandwidths = []
centroids = []
for spec in spectrograms:
    # Calculate the mean spectral bandwidth
    this_mean_bandwidth = np.mean(lr.feature.spectral_bandwidth(S=spec))
    # Calculate the mean spectral centroid
    this_mean_centroid = np.mean(lr.feature.spectral_centroid(S=spec))
    # Collect the values
    bandwidths.append(this_mean_bandwidth)
    centroids.append(this_mean_centroid)
